I MADE A WRONG INFERENCE AND THE USER CAUGHT IT MID-EDIT.

Lines 17/18 repeated verbatim. Particles were already pure white (verified
token AND rendered pixels), the per-letter glows were already gone, so I
reasoned: the only warm source left in the hero is the SideRays beam, therefore
'still dim yellow / silvery white is what I want' must be about the beam. I
switched its hue to silver. 'keep beam yellow. revert and continue.'

The reasoning was sound and the conclusion was wrong, which is the interesting
part. What I actually did was treat a repeated line as necessarily UNSATISFIED
and go hunting for a new referent. The standing instruction is 'if it's on
there, go through it' — that means re-check it, not re-interpret it until it
points somewhere new. A line can repeat because the file was pasted forward.

Rule going forward: when a repeated note has already been verifiably addressed,
say so and ask, rather than widening the referent to whatever is left nearby.
Widening scope silently is how a satisfied request becomes an unwanted change.

